recipe.prompt:
Write the copy for a market report on {{ account.name }}. {{ sender.name | default: 'The sender' }} sends it to {{ person.first_name | default: 'the account' }}. It reads like a short research report: plain, specific, one person writing to one person.

Inputs. The market: {{ data.scope | json }}. The counts below come from company profiles and hiring data and are the only numbers you may use.
- All companies: {{ data.total | json }}
- Companies by segment: {{ data.segments | json }}
- Companies by region: {{ data.regions | json }}
- People in each buying role at the smaller companies: {{ data.roles_small | json }}
- People in each buying role at the larger companies: {{ data.roles_large | json }}
- Growth signals, as counts of companies: {{ data.signals | json }}
- Companies hiring, by segment: {{ data.hiring | json }}
- Companies by size band in the account's region: {{ data.bands | json }}
- Companies hiring, by size band: {{ data.band_hiring | json }}
- The filters behind each count: {{ data.total_defs | json }} {{ data.segment_defs | json }} {{ data.region_defs | json }} {{ data.role_small_defs | json }} {{ data.signal_defs | json }} {{ data.band_defs | json }}
The account: {{ account.name }}, {{ account.industry | default: 'industry unknown' }}, {{ account.size_band | default: 'size unknown' }} employees. Our research: {{ account.dossier.summary | default: 'none' }}

Numbers: write a number only when it is copied exactly from the counts above, as digits with thousands commas (4,170). Do no arithmetic: no percentages, ratios, differences, sums, "one in three" or "half". You may say which count is largest, smallest or bigger than another, and you may say "most" or "a few" when the order of the counts shows it. Name the account's own region and size band when the counts cover them. Never invent customers, news, quotes, years or dates. If a count list above is empty or null, leave out everything about it and use an empty string for its text.

Return JSON with these keys:
- headline: the title of the report, at most 64 characters, sentence case, no year, no colon. It names the market, like "The mid-market freight operator, in numbers".
- stand: 1 or 2 sentences, at most 240 characters, under the title: what the report counts (the total companies and the size range from scope) and that it shows where {{ account.name }} sits.
- brief: 5 findings (4 if people or bands are missing), each {figure, text}. figure is one count copied from the data, like "4,170". text continues after the figure in one or two sentences, at most 220 characters, and never repeats the figure. Cover the total, the largest region, the buyers, the growth signals and the account's size band.
- narrative: {segments, segments_after, regions, regions_after, buyers, buyers_after, signals, signals_after, peers, peers_after}. Each section has a lead paragraph before its chart (2 to 4 sentences, at most 560 characters: what the chart shows and how to read it) and an after paragraph (1 or 2 sentences, at most 360 characters: the one thing that stands out and what it means for {{ account.name }}). buyers texts are empty when there are no role counts, and peers texts are empty when there are no size band counts.
- plays: exactly 3 plays {title, body} for {{ account.name }}, built on the counts. title is at most 70 characters and states the move. body is 2 or 3 sentences, at most 420 characters, quotes the counts it rests on, and says who to reach and why now. Leave out calls or meetings with us; the report ends with that.
- method: 3 to 5 sentences, at most 900 characters, saying how the counts were made, from the filters above: the companies (region, size, industries), the people (current titles at those companies), what hiring means, what raised means and, when there are size band counts, the peer group. Say the counts come from company profiles and hiring data at the time the page was filled. Never claim more than the filters say. Never name the database or the tool the counts come from, in any string.

Voice: short sentences a person would say out loud to a peer. Sentence case for titles.

Rules for every string: no em dashes or en dashes. No exclamation marks. No email addresses or phone numbers. No hype words (unlock, leverage, seamless, robust, crucial, elevate, delve, game changer, cutting edge). Never "real" as emphasis.
